# ASA Hexagon (/hexagon/): text approved by Ahmed; do not edit or add content (CLAUDE.md, "ASA Hexagon").
# The pillar order (1-6) and the balance order are fixed; the corner of each pillar is set in hexagon.py.
HEXAGON = dict(
    title="ASA Hexagon | Six pillars, three balances, mutual trust | ASA",
    desc="The ASA Hexagon: six professional pillars at its corners, three balances formed by pairs of pillars, and trust at the centre. The frame we use to weigh our work.",
    kicker="ASA HEXAGON",
    h1=["Six pillars", "Three balances", "Mutual trust"],
    lead="ASA chose the hexagon as its mark when the firm was founded. To us it is more than a symbol: it is the frame we use to weigh our work. Each corner holds a professional pillar, each pair of pillars forms a balance, and all three balances meet in one purpose: trust.",
    figure="Animated drawing: the six pillars, the three balances, and trust at the centre",
    hint="Hover or tap any corner to see its balance.", replay="Replay",
    trust="Trust",
    why_h="Why a hexagon?",
    why=["\"Hexagon\" comes from two Greek words: <em>hex</em>, six, and <em>gōnía</em>, angle. In Arabic, the word <em>rukn</em> means both a corner and the support a structure stands on. So each of our pillars is a corner, and each corner is something we stand on.",
         "The hexagon holds a special place in geometry. It is one of only three regular shapes that cover a surface completely without gaps, with the triangle and the square, but it divides the surface into equal cells with the least total length of wall. The mathematicians of ancient Alexandria noticed this, and it was proved in 1999 as the \"honeycomb conjecture\". This is why aerospace and other demanding industries use honeycomb structures where maximum strength is needed for minimum weight.",
         "That is what we look for in an audit: a structure with no gaps, and effort placed where it is needed, without waste.",
         "In our mark, the hexagon carries the meaning of the profession itself. Each of its corners holds a pillar: the first is independence, and the other five build on the fundamental principles of the IESBA Code of Ethics: integrity, objectivity, professional behaviour (to which we add quality), confidentiality, and competence and due care. We begin with independence because it is the foundation on which an audit file is built.",
         "The two pillars on each level are held by one balance that keeps them in check: the balance of credibility for independence and quality, the balance of judgement for integrity and objectivity, and the balance of responsibility for confidentiality and competence and due care. The three balances meet at the centre on a single axis where trust is formed: a trust that could not reach its height without all six pillars together.",
         "At this meeting point, the place of trust, the shape divides into two halves, each in the form of the letter A. The upright A at the top is accounting: it starts from the smallest building block, the journal entry, at its apex, and widens until it reaches the financial statements at its broad base. The inverted A at the bottom is audit: it starts where accounting ends, as the auditor receives the financial statements at its broad base and then works downwards until reaching its narrowest point: the entry and its evidence.",
         "The two letters hand over to each other base to base, and between them runs the balance of judgement, a band stretching from one tip of the shape to the other. It brings together integrity and objectivity, the foundation of the fieldwork in an audit file, which is why it is the passage that separates accounting from audit. At its heart, the meeting point itself divides into two small halves in the form of the letter A, one upright and one inverted, expressing the trust that audit adds to accounting.",
         "The green quarter on the audit side is the auditor's green review pen: the mark of checking and sign-off."],
    hive_h="What we learn from the hive",
    hive_p="The hexagonal cell is the work of bees, and the hive offers lessons we try to follow:",
    hive=[dict(name="Guarding the entrance", text="Guard bees check every arrival before it enters. We begin every engagement by assessing acceptance and independence, before any work starts."),
          dict(name="Shared walls", text="Each cell shares its walls with the six around it; no cell stands alone. Our work rests on a team that consults and reviews one another."),
          dict(name="Economy of effort", text="Bees build with the least wax possible. We direct audit effort to where the real risks are."),
          dict(name="No cell is sealed before it is ready", text="Bees cap a cell only when its contents have matured. We sign an opinion only when the evidence is complete.")],
    pillars_h="The six pillars",
    pillars_p="Our pillars build on the five fundamental principles of the Code of Ethics issued by the International Ethics Standards Board for Accountants (IESBA), with quality paired with professional behaviour, plus independence, which every audit engagement requires. In an auditor's work in particular, independence is the foundation: an opinion that does not come from an independent auditor cannot be relied on, however strong the other principles.",
    work="In our work:",
    pillars=[dict(name="Independence", define="An opinion that is free in fact and in appearance, unaffected by any interest or relationship.",
                  practice="we assess independence before accepting every engagement and reassess it in every cycle."),
             dict(name="Quality & professional behaviour", define="Complying with laws and standards and avoiding anything that discredits the profession, within a system of quality management under ISQM 1.",
                  practice="each engagement receives a level of oversight set by its risks, including, where required, an independent review before the report is issued."),
             dict(name="Integrity", define="Being straightforward and honest in every professional relationship: never associated with information we know to be misleading, and never silent where something must be said.",
                  practice="what we write in our reports is what the evidence supports, without embellishment or understatement."),
             dict(name="Objectivity", define="Not allowing bias, conflicts of interest or undue influence to compromise professional judgement.",
                  practice="we weigh corroborating and contradicting evidence together, and document the basis of every significant judgement."),
             dict(name="Confidentiality", define="Protecting client information: disclosing it only with authority or where the law or the profession requires, and never using it for personal advantage.",
                  practice="client files are kept under defined access controls, and client information is used only for its purpose."),
             dict(name="Competence & due care", define="Attaining and maintaining the knowledge and skill required, and acting diligently in line with applicable professional standards.",
                  practice="each engagement is assigned to a team qualified for it, and we prepare for new standards before they take effect.")],
    balances_h="The three balances",
    balances_p="The pillars do not work alone. The two pillars on each level form a balance, each keeping the other from tipping:",
    # pair: the heading after the colon, as shown with the balance (capitalised on its own line)
    balances=[dict(name="Balance of credibility", pair="Independence & quality",
                   text="Independence is not enough without quality, and quality is not enough without independence. Together they give an opinion its credibility. This balance governs the start of every engagement: acceptance and planning."),
              dict(name="Balance of judgement", pair="Integrity & objectivity",
                   text="Integrity is honesty in what we say; objectivity is neutrality in how we weigh what we see. Together they form professional judgement. This balance governs execution and the gathering of evidence."),
              dict(name="Balance of responsibility", pair="Confidentiality & competence and due care",
                   text="Confidentiality is our responsibility to the client for what it entrusts to us; due care is our responsibility to everyone who relies on our work. This balance governs completion, reporting and what follows.")],
    stages="Each balance also accompanies a stage of the engagement: credibility at client acceptance and planning, judgement while the work is performed and the evidence is evaluated, and responsibility at completion and when the report is issued.",
    close="The three balances rest on a single axis: <strong>trust</strong>. The client's trust in our work, and the trust of investors, regulators and every user of financial statements in our opinion.",
    practice_h="ASA Hexagon: the firm's quality management framework",
    practice=["ASA Hexagon is our firm's internal quality management framework. It is built on the International Standard on Quality Management ISQM 1, complemented by ISQM 2 for engagement quality reviews and by the International Standard on Auditing ISA 220 at the level of each engagement. Ultimate responsibility for the system rests with the Managing Partner.",
              "The framework is being implemented in stages, at three levels: preventive, built into the working papers of every engagement from client acceptance to the issued report; detective, through periodic inspection of completed files; and corrective, addressing the causes of any deficiency found and following up on the remediation, with its operational responsibilities assigned to named people within the firm. The aim is for every working file to carry its control signature: a documented trail of every review and sign-off, traceable from the statements down to the entry and its evidence.",
              "Because standards and laws evolve, we review the framework periodically and update it in line with the standards applicable in Egypt and with international standards."],
    began_h="Where it began",
    began="The hexagon was chosen when the firm was founded in May 2022, and its meaning has grown with us year after year.",
    refs="References: IESBA International Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants, including International Independence Standards; ISQM 1; Thomas C. Hales, \"The Honeycomb Conjecture\" (1999).",
)

